    tags: ["moment coefficients", "Gamma function", "Gaussian"],
    workedSolution: {
      steps: [
        { stepNumber: 1, description: "Substitute \\( u=t^2 \\).", workingLatex: "u = t^2, \\quad du = 2t\\,dt, \\quad t = u^{1/2}.", explanation: "The Gaussian weight \\( e^{-t^2} \\) is not a Gamma integral as it stands, because the Gamma template needs the linear exponent \\( e^{-u} \\); the substitution \\( u=t^2 \\) linearises the quadratic exponent. Differentiating gives \\( du=2t\\,dt \\), and the inverse \\( t=u^{1/2} \\) (positive root, since \\( t\\ge0 \\)) will be needed to convert both the power \\( t^n \\) and the differential." },
        { stepNumber: 2, description: "Express \\( dt \\) in terms of \\( du \\).", workingLatex: "dt = \\frac{du}{2t} = \\frac{du}{2u^{1/2}}.", explanation: "Rearrange \\( du=2t\\,dt \\) to \\( dt=du/(2t) \\), then replace \\( t=u^{1/2} \\). The factor \\( u^{-1/2} \\) generated in the denominator is exactly what shifts the eventual Gamma argument to a half-integer — the structural reason Gaussian moments involve \\( \\Gamma \\) of half-integers and hence \\( \\sqrt\\pi \\)." },
        { stepNumber: 3, description: "Rewrite the integrand in \\( u \\).", workingLatex: "a_n = \\int_0^\\infty (u^{1/2})^n e^{-u}\\,\\frac{du}{2u^{1/2}} = \\int_0^\\infty u^{n/2} e^{-u}\\,\\frac{du}{2u^{1/2}}.", explanation: "Substitute \\( t^n=(u^{1/2})^n=u^{n/2} \\), \\( e^{-t^2}=e^{-u} \\), and the \\( dt \\) from step 2. The limits \\( t:0\\to\\infty \\) map to \\( u:0\\to\\infty \\) unchanged, since \\( u=t^2 \\) is increasing on \\( [0,\\infty) \\) and fixes \\( 0\\mapsto0 \\)." },
        { stepNumber: 4, description: "Combine the powers of \\( u \\).", workingLatex: "a_n = \\frac{1}{2}\\int_0^\\infty u^{n/2 - 1/2} e^{-u}\\,du = \\frac{1}{2}\\int_0^\\infty u^{(n-1)/2} e^{-u}\\,du.", explanation: "Merge the powers via \\( u^{n/2}/u^{1/2}=u^{(n-1)/2} \\) (subtract exponents) and pull the constant \\( 1/2 \\) outside the integral. We now have a single power of \\( u \\) times \\( e^{-u} \\) — the Gamma form, ready to read off." },
        { stepNumber: 5, description: "Identify the Gamma integral.", workingLatex: "a_n = \\frac{1}{2}\\,\\Gamma\\!\\left(\\frac{n+1}{2}\\right).", explanation: "Match the exponent to \\( t^{z-1} \\): \\( (n-1)/2=\\tfrac{n+1}{2}-1 \\), so \\( z=\\tfrac{n+1}{2} \\) and the integral is \\( \\Gamma\\bigl(\\tfrac{n+1}{2}\\bigr) \\). The half-integer argument is the unmistakable signature of a Gaussian moment, and it makes the even and odd moments behave quite differently (even \\( n \\) gives half-integer \\( \\Gamma \\) with \\( \\sqrt\\pi \\); odd \\( n \\) gives integer \\( \\Gamma \\), a plain factorial)." },
        { stepNumber: 6, description: "Evaluate \\( a_0 \\).", workingLatex: "a_0 = \\frac{1}{2}\\Gamma\\!\\left(\\tfrac{0+1}{2}\\right) = \\frac{1}{2}\\Gamma\\!\\left(\\tfrac12\\right) = \\frac{\\sqrt\\pi}{2}.", explanation: "Set \\( n=0 \\), giving argument \\( 1/2 \\), and use the special value \\( \\Gamma(\\tfrac12)=\\sqrt\\pi \\). This recovers the classic Gaussian integral \\( \\int_0^\\infty e^{-t^2}\\,dt=\\sqrt\\pi/2 \\) — a decisive sanity check that the whole substitution and the half-integer bookkeeping are correct." },
        { stepNumber: 7, description: "Evaluate \\( a_1 \\) and gauge the growth.", workingLatex: "a_1 = \\frac{1}{2}\\Gamma\\!\\left(\\tfrac{1+1}{2}\\right) = \\frac{1}{2}\\Gamma(1) = \\frac{1}{2}; \\quad \\int_0^\\infty t e^{-t^2}\\,dt = \\left[-\\tfrac12 e^{-t^2}\\right]_0^\\infty = \\frac12.", explanation: "Set \\( n=1 \\), giving argument \\( 1 \\), and use \\( \\Gamma(1)=1 \\); the direct elementary integral \\( \\int_0^\\infty te^{-t^2}\\,dt=1/2 \\) confirms it independently. The first two moments \\( a_0=\\sqrt\\pi/2\\approx0.886 \\) and \\( a_1=0.5 \\) happen to dip, but the moments soon grow: \\( a_{n+1}/a_n=\\Gamma(\\tfrac{n+2}{2})/\\Gamma(\\tfrac{n+1}{2})\\sim\\sqrt{n/2}\\to\\infty \\), so this Stieltjes series also has radius of convergence \\( 0 \\) and diverges. The growth is sub-factorial — slower than the \\( n! \\) of \\( e^{-t} \\) — but still unbounded; the lighter Gaussian tail tempers the moments without taming them to convergence, unlike the genuinely bounded support of Q6 and Q10." },
      ],
      finalAnswer: "\\( a_n=\\tfrac12\\Gamma\\!\\bigl(\\tfrac{n+1}{2}\\bigr) \\); \\( a_0=\\tfrac{\\sqrt\\pi}{2},\\ a_1=\\tfrac12 \\).",
      canonicalAnswer: "gamma((n+1)/2)/2",
    },
