      "Consider \\( H(x)=\\int_0^\\infty \\dfrac{\\rho(t)}{1+xt}\\,dt \\) with \\( \\rho(t)=\\dfrac{1}{\\pi}\\,\\dfrac{1}{1+t^2} \\cdot 2 \\) on \\( [0,\\infty) \\) (so \\( \\int_0^\\infty\\rho=1 \\)). Show that the formal moments \\( a_n=\\int_0^\\infty t^n\\rho(t)\\,dt \\) DIVERGE for \\( n\\ge 1 \\), and explain what this means for the existence of a power-series asymptotic expansion of \\( H \\).",
    marks: 5,
    examStyle: true,
    yearCreated: 2026,
    tags: ["heavy-tailed density", "moment divergence"],
    workedSolution: {
      steps: [
        { stepNumber: 1, description: "Write the moment integral.", workingLatex: "a_n = \\int_0^\\infty t^n\\,\\frac{2}{\\pi}\\,\\frac{1}{1+t^2}\\,dt = \\frac{2}{\\pi}\\int_0^\\infty \\frac{t^n}{1+t^2}\\,dt.", explanation: "Insert the density \\( \\rho=\\tfrac{2}{\\pi}\\tfrac{1}{1+t^2} \\) (the half-Cauchy weight) and pull the normalisation constant \\( 2/\\pi \\) outside. Whether the moment exists is decided entirely by the convergence of \\( \\int_0^\\infty t^n/(1+t^2)\\,dt \\); the constant cannot rescue a divergent integral." },
        { stepNumber: 2, description: "Examine the large-\\( t \\) behaviour.", workingLatex: "\\frac{t^n}{1+t^2} \\sim \\frac{t^n}{t^2} = t^{\\,n-2} \\quad (t\\to\\infty).", explanation: "Convergence of an integral over \\( [0,\\infty) \\) is a question about the tail (the integrand is continuous, hence integrable, on any finite interval). For large \\( t \\) the \\( +1 \\) is negligible against \\( t^2 \\), so the integrand decays only like the power \\( t^{n-2} \\) — a heavy power-law tail, in stark contrast to the exponential decay \\( e^{-t} \\) that made every earlier moment finite." },
        { stepNumber: 3, description: "Recall the power test at infinity.", workingLatex: "\\int^\\infty t^{p}\\,dt \\text{ converges} \\iff p < -1.", explanation: "A power \\( t^p \\) is integrable near infinity exactly when \\( p<-1 \\), so that the antiderivative \\( t^{p+1}/(p+1) \\) tends to a finite limit as \\( t\\to\\infty \\) (for \\( p=-1 \\) it is \\( \\ln t\\to\\infty \\), the borderline failure). This is the standard \\( p \\)-test for tail convergence, the continuous analogue of the \\( \\sum n^{-p} \\) test." },
        { stepNumber: 4, description: "Apply the test with \\( p=n-2 \\).", workingLatex: "p = n-2 < -1 \\iff n < 1.", explanation: "Here the tail exponent is \\( p=n-2 \\), so convergence at infinity requires \\( n-2<-1 \\), i.e. \\( n<1 \\) — only \\( n=0 \\) qualifies. For every \\( n\\ge1 \\) the tail decays no faster than \\( t^{-1} \\) and the integral diverges at the upper limit; the integrand is fine at \\( t=0 \\), so the divergence is purely a tail effect." },
        { stepNumber: 5, description: "Conclude on the moments.", workingLatex: "a_n = +\\infty \\ (n\\ge 1); \\qquad a_0 = \\frac{2}{\\pi}\\int_0^\\infty\\frac{dt}{1+t^2}=\\frac{2}{\\pi}\\Bigl[\\arctan t\\Bigr]_0^\\infty=\\frac{2}{\\pi}\\cdot\\frac{\\pi}{2}=1.", explanation: "Only the zeroth moment is finite, and it equals the normalisation \\( \\int\\rho=1 \\) (via \\( \\int_0^\\infty dt/(1+t^2)=[\\arctan t]_0^\\infty=\\pi/2 \\)). All higher moments are infinite because the tail is too fat to integrate against any positive power of \\( t \\) — the density has no finite mean, variance, or any higher moment, the hallmark of a heavy-tailed law." },
        { stepNumber: 6, description: "Interpret for the expansion.", workingLatex: "H(x) \\not\\sim \\sum_{n\\ge0} (-1)^n a_n x^n; \\qquad H(x) = 1 + \\frac{2}{\\pi}x\\ln x + O(x) \\ (x\\to0^+).", explanation: "With \\( a_n=\\infty \\) for \\( n\\ge1 \\), the naive Stieltjes series simply has no coefficients beyond \\( a_0 \\); the integer-power expansion breaks down at first order. The true small-\\( x \\) behaviour is governed by the tail of \\( \\rho \\) and carries a logarithm, \\( H(x)=1+\\tfrac{2}{\\pi}x\\ln x+\\cdots \\) (numerically confirmed: the \\( x\\ln x \\) term matches \\( H \\) at \\( x=10^{-2},10^{-3} \\)). The lesson, made precise in Q27, is that the clean integer-power series of the earlier questions depended essentially on \\( \\rho \\) decaying fast enough for all moments to exist." },
      ],
      finalAnswer: "\\( a_0=1 \\) but \\( a_n=\\infty \\) for \\( n\\ge1 \\); the heavy tail destroys the integer-power asymptotic series (logarithmic / fractional-power terms appear instead).",
    },
