    questionText:
      "For \\( \\rho(t)=e^{-t} \\), define optimal truncation by \\( N+1=[1/x] \\) (integer part). Using the error bound \\( \\mathrm{Err}_N\\le (N+1)!\\,x^{N+1} \\) and Stirling's formula, show that the optimal error is \\( O\\!\\bigl([1/x]^{1/2}e^{-[1/x]}\\bigr)=o(x^M) \\) as \\( x\\to0^+ \\) for every positive integer \\( M \\).",
    marks: 6,
    examStyle: true,
    yearCreated: 2026,
    tags: ["optimal truncation", "Stirling", "superasymptotic"],
    workedSolution: {
      steps: [
        { stepNumber: 1, description: "Set the truncation point.", workingLatex: "N+1 = \\left[\\frac1x\\right] =: M_x, \\quad \\text{so } x \\approx \\frac{1}{M_x}.", explanation: "The smallest term of \\( F \\)'s series sits at \\( n\\approx1/x \\) (Q12), so the optimal truncation keeps terms up to \\( N \\) with \\( N+1=[1/x] \\), the first omitted index. Naming this index \\( M_x \\) and recording \\( x\\approx1/M_x \\) keeps the algebra readable, and the approximation \\( x=1/M_x \\) (exact up to the integer part) is what drives the cancellation later." },
        { stepNumber: 2, description: "Insert into the error bound.", workingLatex: "\\mathrm{Err}_N \\le (N+1)!\\,x^{N+1} = M_x!\\,x^{M_x}.", explanation: "Use the rigorous alternating-series bound from Q11 — the error is at most the first omitted term \\( (N+1)!\\,x^{N+1} \\) — evaluated at the optimal index \\( N+1=M_x \\). The estimate is now purely a matter of sizing \\( M_x!\\,x^{M_x} \\); the analysis has been reduced to one factorial-times-power." },
        { stepNumber: 3, description: "Apply Stirling to the factorial.", workingLatex: "M_x! \\sim \\sqrt{2\\pi M_x}\\,\\left(\\frac{M_x}{e}\\right)^{M_x}.", explanation: "Stirling's approximation \\( M!\\sim\\sqrt{2\\pi M}(M/e)^M \\) is exactly the right tool at large \\( M_x \\) (and \\( M_x=[1/x]\\to\\infty \\) as \\( x\\to0^+ \\)). The factorial splits into an algebraic prefactor \\( \\sqrt{2\\pi M_x} \\) and the dominant \\( (M_x/e)^{M_x} \\); it is the latter that will collide with \\( x^{M_x} \\)." },
        { stepNumber: 4, description: "Substitute \\( x^{M_x}=M_x^{-M_x} \\).", workingLatex: "M_x!\\,x^{M_x} \\sim \\sqrt{2\\pi M_x}\\,\\left(\\frac{M_x}{e}\\right)^{M_x}\\left(\\frac{1}{M_x}\\right)^{M_x}.", explanation: "Since \\( x\\approx1/M_x \\), the power \\( x^{M_x}=(1/M_x)^{M_x}=M_x^{-M_x} \\). Placing it beside Stirling exposes the impending cancellation: the enormous \\( M_x^{M_x} \\) from the factorial is about to be annihilated by the equally enormous \\( M_x^{-M_x} \\) from the power — the precise tuning of \\( x \\) to \\( 1/M_x \\) is what makes this happen." },
        { stepNumber: 5, description: "Cancel the powers of \\( M_x \\).", workingLatex: "= \\sqrt{2\\pi M_x}\\,\\frac{M_x^{M_x}}{e^{M_x}}\\,M_x^{-M_x} = \\sqrt{2\\pi M_x}\\; e^{-M_x}.", explanation: "The \\( M_x^{M_x} \\) from Stirling and \\( M_x^{-M_x} \\) from \\( x^{M_x} \\) cancel exactly, leaving only \\( e^{-M_x} \\) times the algebraic prefactor \\( \\sqrt{2\\pi M_x} \\). The colossal factorial and the tiny power have conspired to produce a single exponentially small factor — this cancellation is the entire mechanism behind beyond-all-orders accuracy." },
        { stepNumber: 6, description: "Express in \\( x \\) and bound below all orders.", workingLatex: "\\sqrt{2\\pi M_x}\\,e^{-M_x} = O\\!\\left(M_x^{1/2} e^{-M_x}\\right) = \\Bigl[\\tfrac1x\\Bigr]^{1/2}e^{-[1/x]} = o(x^M)\\ \\forall M\\in\\mathbb{Z}^+.", explanation: "Substitute \\( M_x=[1/x] \\). The exponentially small \\( e^{-1/x} \\) beats every algebraic power \\( x^M \\) as \\( x\\to0^+ \\), since \\( e^{-1/x}/x^M=e^{-1/x+M\\ln(1/x)}\\to0 \\) (the linear \\( 1/x \\) dominates the logarithm). So the optimal error is smaller than \\( x^M \\) for EVERY \\( M \\): optimal truncation achieves superasymptotic (beyond-all-orders) accuracy, qualitatively better than any fixed-order truncation, which only gives \\( O(x^N) \\). The \\( e^{-1/x} \\) floor is itself the size of the exponentially-small terms Borel summation (Q26) would need to capture to go further still." },
      ],
      finalAnswer: "Optimal error \\( = O\\!\\bigl([1/x]^{1/2}e^{-[1/x]}\\bigr)=o(x^M) \\) for every \\( M \\) — exponentially small, beyond all algebraic orders.",
    },
