    difficulty: "Challenge",
    answerType: "expression",
    questionText:
      "Use integration by parts on \\( E_1(x)=\\int_x^\\infty \\dfrac{e^{-t}}{t}\\,dt \\) to derive its asymptotic expansion \\( E_1(x)\\sim e^{-x}\\bigl(\\tfrac1x-\\tfrac1{x^2}+\\tfrac{2}{x^3}-\\cdots\\bigr) \\) as \\( x\\to\\infty \\), showing the remainder after \\( N \\) terms is \\( O(e^{-x}x^{-N-1}) \\), and connect this to the Stieltjes form \\( F=\\tfrac{e^{1/x}}{x}E_1(1/x) \\).",
    marks: 6,
    examStyle: true,
    yearCreated: 2026,
    tags: ["exponential integral", "integration by parts", "remainder"],
    workedSolution: {
      steps: [
        { stepNumber: 1, description: "Choose the parts.", workingLatex: "u = t^{-1}, \\quad dv = e^{-t}\\,dt \\implies du = -t^{-2}\\,dt, \\quad v = -e^{-t}.", explanation: "The integrand \\( e^{-t}/t \\) has no elementary antiderivative, so instead of evaluating \\( E_1 \\) in closed form we integrate by parts repeatedly to generate an asymptotic series. The choice is deliberate: differentiating \\( u=t^{-1} \\) lowers its power to \\( t^{-2} \\) (each pass makes the leftover integral smaller for large \\( t \\)), while integrating \\( dv=e^{-t}\\,dt \\) is trivial and reproduces \\( e^{-t} \\)." },
        { stepNumber: 2, description: "Apply the formula once.", workingLatex: "E_1(x) = \\left[-t^{-1}e^{-t}\\right]_x^\\infty - \\int_x^\\infty (-e^{-t})(-t^{-2})\\,dt = \\left[-t^{-1}e^{-t}\\right]_x^\\infty - \\int_x^\\infty t^{-2}e^{-t}\\,dt.", explanation: "Use \\( \\int u\\,dv=uv-\\int v\\,du \\) with \\( uv=-t^{-1}e^{-t} \\). In \\( -\\int v\\,du \\) the integrand \\( v\\,du=(-e^{-t})(-t^{-2}\\,dt)=t^{-2}e^{-t}\\,dt \\) is positive (two minus signs multiply to a plus), so the term is \\( -\\int_x^\\infty t^{-2}e^{-t}\\,dt \\). Showing the unsimplified \\( v\\,du \\) first makes the double-sign bookkeeping unambiguous." },
        { stepNumber: 3, description: "Evaluate the boundary term.", workingLatex: "\\left[-t^{-1}e^{-t}\\right]_x^\\infty = 0 - \\left(-\\frac{e^{-x}}{x}\\right) = \\frac{e^{-x}}{x} \\implies E_1(x) = \\frac{e^{-x}}{x} - \\int_x^\\infty \\frac{e^{-t}}{t^2}\\,dt.", explanation: "At \\( t\\to\\infty \\), \\( t^{-1}e^{-t}\\to0 \\) (the exponential dominates); at \\( t=x \\) the bracket is \\( -x^{-1}e^{-x} \\), and subtracting the lower-limit value gives \\( +e^{-x}/x \\). The first asymptotic term \\( e^{-x}/x \\) has appeared, with the next-order correction sitting in the smaller integral \\( \\int t^{-2}e^{-t} \\)." },
        { stepNumber: 4, description: "Iterate the integration by parts.", workingLatex: "E_1(x) = e^{-x}\\left(\\frac{1}{x} - \\frac{1}{x^2} + \\frac{2!}{x^3} - \\cdots + (-1)^{N-1}\\frac{(N-1)!}{x^N}\\right) + R_N.", explanation: "Repeat the same parts on \\( \\int t^{-k}e^{-t}\\,dt \\): integrating \\( e^{-t} \\) and differentiating \\( t^{-k}\\to-k\\,t^{-k-1} \\) peels off the next boundary term \\( (-1)^{k-1}(k-1)!/x^k \\) and leaves a smaller integral, the coefficient picking up a factor \\( k-1 \\) each round so the factorials \\( (k-1)! \\) build up. The factorial numerators with the geometric \\( x^{-k} \\) foreshadow that this series, like the Stieltjes one, diverges for fixed \\( x \\)." },
        { stepNumber: 5, description: "Write the remainder.", workingLatex: "R_N = (-1)^N N!\\int_x^\\infty \\frac{e^{-t}}{t^{N+1}}\\,dt.", explanation: "After \\( N \\) integrations by parts the leftover integral carries the accumulated factorial \\( N! \\) (from the product of the differentiation factors \\( 1\\cdot2\\cdots N \\)) and the power \\( t^{-(N+1)} \\). This single explicit integral is the exact remainder, so bounding it gives a rigorous error control rather than a heuristic one." },
        { stepNumber: 6, description: "Bound the remainder.", workingLatex: "|R_N| \\le \\frac{N!}{x^{N+1}}\\int_x^\\infty e^{-t}\\,dt = \\frac{N!\\,e^{-x}}{x^{N+1}} = O\\!\\bigl(e^{-x}x^{-N-1}\\bigr).", explanation: "On \\( [x,\\infty) \\) the decreasing factor \\( t^{-(N+1)} \\) attains its maximum at the left endpoint \\( t=x \\), namely \\( x^{-(N+1)} \\); pulling that constant out leaves \\( \\int_x^\\infty e^{-t}\\,dt=e^{-x} \\). The remainder \\( O(e^{-x}x^{-N-1}) \\) is smaller by a factor \\( 1/x \\) than the last term kept (\\( \\sim e^{-x}(N-1)!\\,x^{-N} \\)) — precisely the Poincaré asymptotic property, here for an expansion in \\( 1/x \\) as \\( x\\to\\infty \\)." },
        { stepNumber: 7, description: "Connect to the Stieltjes integral.", workingLatex: "F(x)=\\frac{e^{1/x}}{x}E_1\\!\\bigl(\\tfrac1x\\bigr) \\sim \\frac{e^{1/x}}{x}\\cdot e^{-1/x}\\,x\\bigl(1-x+2x^2-\\cdots\\bigr) = 1-x+2x^2-\\cdots.", explanation: "Set the argument to \\( 1/x \\) in the \\( E_1 \\) series: the prefactor \\( e^{-1/x}/(1/x)=e^{-1/x}x \\), and each \\( 1/(1/x)^k=x^k \\), so the bracket becomes \\( 1-x+2x^2-\\cdots \\). Multiplying by \\( e^{1/x}/x \\) cancels the exponentials (\\( e^{1/x}e^{-1/x}=1 \\)) and the \\( x \\), reproducing the Stieltjes divergent series with the same coefficients \\( (-1)^n n! \\). So the IBP route here and the substitution route of Q19 deliver one and the same expansion — two derivations of identical content, a strong internal consistency check." },
      ],
      finalAnswer: "\\( E_1(x)\\sim e^{-x}\\sum_{n=0}^\\infty \\dfrac{(-1)^n n!}{x^{n+1}} \\), remainder \\( O(e^{-x}x^{-N-1}) \\); via \\( F=\\tfrac{e^{1/x}}{x}E_1(1/x) \\) this is exactly \\( \\sum(-1)^n n! x^n \\).",
    },
